{"title":"Power Plant Engineer Roadmap","role":"Power Plant Engineer","summary":"An Electrical Engineering degree is strongly aligned with power-plant engineering. The field combines electrical machines, power systems, generation, protection, instrumentation, control systems, maintenance and industrial safety.","facts":{"Starts":"Year 1 of UG","Current degree":"B.Tech / B.E. (Bachelor of Technology / Engineering)","Timeline assumption":"Four-year B.Tech/B.E. beginning in 2026 and graduating around 2030."},"about":[{"label":"Career-route check","text":"Power Plant Engineer is a realistic graduate-entry career. Common entry positions include Graduate Engineer Trainee, Electrical Engineer, Plant Engineer, Operations Engineer, Maintenance Engineer, Protection Engineer and Electrical Projects Engineer."}],"intro":[],"horizons":[{"period":"2026–2030","question":"What can I enter now?","output":"power-sector internships, generation-plant training, electrical maintenance and engineering trainee positions"},{"period":"2030–2035","question":"How should I specialise?","output":"plant operations, electrical systems, protection, maintenance, generation technology or control systems"},{"period":"2035–2040","question":"How should I evolve?","output":"plant engineering leadership, reliability, asset management, grid integration and efficiency"},{"period":"2040–2046","question":"How can I remain relevant?","output":"renewable integration, battery storage, smart grids, digital power plants, predictive maintenance and low-carbon generation"}],"sections":[{"heading":"Overview","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Power Plant Engineers can work in thermal, hydroelectric, nuclear, solar, wind, gas and other generation facilities. The exact technical pathway depends heavily on the generation technology."}]},{"heading":"What to build each year","kind":"build","blocks":[{"type":"table","header":["When","Build this","Learn from","Proof of progress","Gap it moves"],"rows":[[{"text":"Y